WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.829
This video uses the Human Mapping and Variants demo project, the version with results.

00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:13.477
Choose Help, then Demo Projects, and click Download and Open beside it.

00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:16.422
The video Your first project shows each step.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.497
A pileup shows aligned reads stacked at their positions on the reference.

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:25.293
Under Analyses, open the minimap2 result.

00:00:25.293 --> 00:00:28.770
minimap2 is the read aligner that placed each read.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.883
Select its row and click Focus.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:35.452
The curve shows read depth, the number of reads covering each base.

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:37.633
Here the mean depth is about 45.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:45.021
Type positions 2,050 to 2,350 into the position field above the viewer and press Return.

00:00:45.021 --> 00:00:46.666
Each read is now a bar.

00:00:46.666 --> 00:00:52.528
Bases that match the reference are drawn plain, and bases that differ are colored.

00:00:52.528 --> 00:00:56.603
A colored base in one read is usually a sequencing error.

00:00:56.603 --> 00:01:00.392
A column where many reads differ is a likely variant.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:10.252
Most human chromosomes come in two copies, one from each parent, and the version of a base on each copy is an allele.

00:01:10.252 --> 00:01:18.640
At position 2,078 of this slice, every read has A where the reference has G, so both copies most likely carry A.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.786
This is homozygous alternate, written 1/1.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:31.499
At 2,162, 28 of 51 reads have T instead of A, so one copy most likely carries T and the other the reference A.

00:01:31.499 --> 00:01:38.309
This is heterozygous, written 0/1, where 0 is the reference allele and 1 the alternate.

00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:46.683
The Variants tab below the viewer lists the variants a variant-calling program found, with their genotypes.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.567
To learn more, read the manual chapter Reading an Alignment.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.722
More videos are on the Videos page of the website.
